Feature flag short-circuits the entire `disabledTxTypes` check, not just lending types.

If `disabledTxTypes` ever contains (or gains) non-lending entries, they'll be silently bypassed when `featureLendingProtocolV1_1` is active. The guard should be applied per-type inside the lambda, e.g.:

```cpp
if (std::any_of(
    disabledTxTypes.begin(), disabledTxTypes.end(),
    [&ctx, txType](auto const& disabled) {
        // Skip only lending-specific disabled types when the feature is enabled
        if (ctx.rules.enabled(featureLendingProtocolV1_1) && isLendingTxType(disabled))
            return false;
        return txType == disabled;
    }))
```

This keeps future non-lending entries in `disabledTxTypes` correctly blocked regardless of the lending feature flag.

Right now, `sfRawTransactions` is required for Batch transactions. There's no reason to think that will ever change, but if it does, we could get into a bad situation.

Consider a Batch transaction submitted without a raw transactions field, but with one or more batch signers, under the size limit. `buildBatchTxnIDs()` will exit early, leaving `batchTxnIds_` uninitialized, and then these signature check functions will access an uninitialized`batchTxnIds_` without any checking, which could have bad consequences depending on the `std::optional` implementation.

The chances of this ever happening are obviously very very slim, but I think it would not hurt to add a strictly defensive / future-proofing check here and/or in _both_ `checkBatchMultiSign` and `checkBatchSingleSign`.

```
if (!batchTxnIDs_) {
    // LCOV_EXCL_START
	UNREACHABLE(...);
	return std::unexpected("No inner transactions");
    // LCOV_EXCL_STOP
}
```

My [previous comment](https://github.com/XRPLF/rippled/pull/7736#discussion_r3583066724) noted that
> As long as all the possible failures are also checked in `preflight`, there's no danger that the transaction will succeed, even with such a low base fee.

I don't think that all of these conditions are explicitly checked in `preflight`, and thus I have a concern that a malformed transaction could slip through paying an insufficient fee.

First it's important to note, most of these failures should be impossible under protocol rules, like this one for example. There's no way that `Transactor::calculateBaseFee` is going to return a value large enough that adding one more "base fee" is going to overflow.

Also, with the number of transactions and the number of signers limited, even at their craziest maximum possible individual fees, the total just cannot reasonably reach the overflow value, which would be more XRP than has ever existed. Those limits _are_ enforced in `preflight` and the `STTx` ctor, so the transaction will fail, or fail to even be instantiated.

The only failures that are reasonably possible to create in a transaction are `signers.size() > kMaxBatchSigners` and `nestedSigners.size() > STTx::kMaxMultiSigners`. But those will both get caught in `STTx` signature verification. The tx will never get this far, because it will be improperly signed.

**Conclusion:** Even though the conditions are not _explicitly_ checked in `preflight`, they are _implicitly_ checked in other ways or not reasonably possible. The chances of such a transaction succeeding are extremely low, but I can't guarantee that they're zero for all future scenarios (such as the ledger base fee changing).

**Mitigation:** Here are two suggestions to be extra sure that there is no risk. There are certainly other ways to accomplish the same goal.

1. Implement a `std::optional<XRPAmount> calculateBaseFeeImpl` which moves the existing implementation, but returns `std::nullopt` in the failure cases.
	a. Have `calculateBaseFee` call

        if (auto const ret = calculateBaseFeeImpl(view, tx))
            return *ret;
        return view.fees().base;

    b. Have `preclaim` call

        if (auto const ret = calculateBaseFeeImpl(ctx.view, ctx.tx); !ret.has_value())
            return tecINSUFF_FEE;

2. Along the same lines, but with less refactoring, just have `preclaim` call

        if (auto const ret = calculateBaseFeeImpl(view, tx); ret == view.fees().base)
            return tecINSUFF_FEE;

      The problem with this option is that networks running with 0 base fee (hey, it's possible) will always fail this check.
